post_install() {
  cat <<'EOF'
Ghost is installed. The package does not edit Hyprland or Omarchy
configuration. Optional summon key and bar snippets are under
/usr/share/doc/ghost/shell-contrib/.

To relay the browser you already use, load this directory unpacked in Chromium
(or add it to --load-extension= in ~/.config/chromium-flags.conf), then press
Allow in the HUD when it shows the code from the extension's popup:

  /usr/share/ghost/chromium-extension

Shared notes and tasks are ordinary files in your XDG Documents directory; no
extra application or skill is required.

Enable Ghost for the current graphical login with:

  systemctl --user enable --now ghostd.service
  ln -sfn /usr/share/ghost/plugin ~/.config/omarchy/plugins/ferdousbhai.ghost
  omarchy-shell shell rescanPlugins && omarchy plugin enable ferdousbhai.ghost

The HUD is an omarchy-shell plugin, so it needs no unit of its own; summon it
with `omarchy-shell shell toggle ferdousbhai.ghost`.

Skills, MCP servers, and other pi integrations are yours to install from
upstream as this desktop user; Ghost packages none of them. Many MCP servers
launch through npx, which the optional npm package provides.

Ghost homes, credentials, browser profiles, and daemon state live in your home
directory. Pacman does not own or remove them.
EOF
}

post_upgrade() {
  cat <<'EOF'
Ghost was upgraded. Re-enable and restart the user services so an older
default.target link is replaced by the graphical-session.target lifecycle:

  systemctl --user reenable --now ghostd.service
  omarchy-shell shell rescanPlugins

No files under ~/ghosts, ~/.config/ghost, or ~/.local/state/ghost were changed
by the package upgrade.
EOF
}

pre_remove() {
  cat <<'EOF'
Before pacman removes the unit, stop and disable it from the desktop user's
session in another terminal, and drop the HUD plugin:

  systemctl --user disable --now ghostd.service
  omarchy plugin remove ferdousbhai.ghost --yes

Removal still preserves every user-owned ghost home, credential, browser
profile, and daemon state file.
EOF
}

post_remove() {
  cat <<'EOF'
Ghost's system files were removed. User-owned ghost homes, credentials, browser
profiles, and daemon state were deliberately left in place. If either process
is still running in an existing login, stop it with:

  systemctl --user disable --now ghostd.service
EOF
}
